## Deploying to AWS (Terraform)

Follow the steps in order, from the repository root. Nothing is created in AWS before step 3.

### 1. Install the tools

Install [Terraform](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/install) 1.10 or later (`infra/providers.tf` requires it: the state lock is a file in the bucket, with no DynamoDB table).
{{#if (eq blueprint "lambda-terraform-aws-hono")}}
The `justfile` wraps the Terraform commands below (`just init dev`, `just plan dev`, `just apply dev`, `just output`); install [`just`](https://github.com/casey/just) to use it. The steps call Terraform directly, so they do not need it.
{{/if}}

### 2. Set the credentials and the region

{{> aws/credentials}}

### 3. Create the state bucket

Terraform keeps its state in an S3 bucket that has to exist before `terraform init`. Bucket names are unique across AWS, so this one carries the account id. Versioning keeps the previous states.

```bash
STATE_BUCKET="{{projectName}}-tfstate-$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)"
aws s3 mb "s3://$STATE_BUCKET" --region "$AWS_REGION"
aws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket "$STATE_BUCKET" --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled
```

### 4. Point the backend at the bucket

```bash
sed -i.bak "s/<BUCKET_NAME>/$STATE_BUCKET/" infra/backend/dev.hcl infra/backend/prod.hcl
rm infra/backend/dev.hcl.bak infra/backend/prod.hcl.bak
```

Commit both files: a bucket name is not a secret, and CI reads it from them. They hold no profile and no region on purpose: the S3 backend reads `AWS_PROFILE` and `AWS_REGION` from the environment, so the same files work on your machine (a profile) and in CI (OIDC credentials). Do not add a `profile` or a `region` to them.

{{#if (eq blueprint "lambda-terraform-aws-hono")}}
### 5. Build the functions

Terraform zips the `dist` directory of `apps/api`, `apps/cron` and `apps/worker` (the zips go to `.build/`, which git ignores), so build them first:

```bash
{{runScript "build"}}
```

### 6. Deploy the dev environment

```bash
terraform -chdir=infra init -backend-config=backend/dev.hcl
terraform -chdir=infra apply -var="env=dev"
```

`apply` shows the plan and waits for `yes`. For prod, switch the backend and apply with the prod environment:

```bash
terraform -chdir=infra init -reconfigure -backend-config=backend/prod.hcl
terraform -chdir=infra apply -var="env=prod"
```

### 7. Check the live URL

```bash
API_URL=$(terraform -chdir=infra output -raw api_url)
curl "${API_URL%/}/health/ping"
```

The answer is `{"ok":true,"message":"Health check","data":{"id":"ping"}}`. `output` reads the state of the backend initialized last, so run it right after the `apply` of the environment you want to check.

### 8. Deploy from GitHub Actions

On a push to `main` the workflow deploys `dev`; a manual run deploys `dev` or `prod`. The GitHub environments are named after the Terraform environments.

{{> aws/github-oidc environments="dev prod"}}
{{else}}
### 5. Initialize the backend

```bash
terraform -chdir=infra init -backend-config=backend/dev.hcl
```

`infra/` holds the provider and the backend only: `main.tf` has no resource yet. Add yours, then `terraform -chdir=infra plan -var="env=dev"` shows what `apply` creates. Use `-backend-config=backend/prod.hcl` with `-reconfigure` and `-var="env=prod"` for prod.
{{/if}}
